<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azzone Visconti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1302">1302</a>–<a href="/years/1339">1339</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azzone Visconti was lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from 1329 to 1339. He bought the title of imperial vicar, rebuilt Milan's walls and public buildings, and extended Visconti rule over the neighbouring communes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, including Bergamo, Como, Cremona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>, Novara, Vercelli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> and Piacenza. His conquests turned the lordship of Milan into a regional state that later became a duchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azzone-visconti.jpg" alt="Azzone Visconti" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Azzone_Visconti.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Azzone Visconti was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> on 7 December 1302, the sole legitimate son of Galeazzo I Visconti and Beatrice d'Este. In 1322 he was lord of Piacenza, but in the same year he and his father were forced to flee. In 1325 he commanded troops at the battles of Altopascio and Zappolino, both victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a>. In 1327 Galeazzo and the other leading members of the family were arrested on suspicion of assassinating Galeazzo's younger brother Stefano. The Emperor confiscated their territories, local families took control of many cities long tied to the Viscontis, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> was ruled by a new imperial appointee and a council hostile to them. When Galeazzo died later that year, Visconti power was at a low point.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Azzone at once struggled with his uncle Marco for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. In 1329, with the support of another uncle, Giovanni, he bought the title of imperial vicar of Milan from the emperor Louis IV for 60,000 florins, or 125,000 by another account, of which he paid only 12,000, the feeble Louis being unable to force payment. Marco was killed the same year and Azzone was named as one of the assassins, but he was never condemned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> excommunicated him, placed Milan under interdict and threatened an invasion by his French allies. Azzone submitted and renounced the vicariate, but kept political power in Milan and on 15 March 1330 was appointed signore of Milan.</p><p>In 1331 he married Catherine of Savoy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> and allied with Theodore I, Marquess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert of Anjou</a> in order to capture his possessions in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He conquered Bergamo and Pizzighettone in 1332 and in 1335 went on to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crema">Crema</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> lands that had ceded themselves to the Papal States, as well as Vercelli and Cremona. In 1339 Lodrisio Visconti revolted with a company of hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and Azzone, suffering from gout, had Luchino Visconti command the Milanese army, which defeated Lodrisio at the battle of Parabiago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Azzone died on 16 August 1339 of an attack of gout and was buried in the church of San Gottardo, which he had commissioned some years before. His only child was a daughter, Luchina, and he had no male heir, so he was succeeded as lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> by his uncles Giovanni and Luchino. Besides his political and military career, he is remembered for his great construction works in Milan and other cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Azzone Visconti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1332 <b>Azzone Visconti</b> took Bergamo into the Lordship of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-bergamo">Commune of Bergamo</a></li><li><blockquote>Exhausted by faction, Brescia accepted the Scaliger lords of Verona and then in 1337 submitted to <b>Azzone Visconti</b>, joining the Lordship of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-brescia">Commune of Brescia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1335 Franchino Rusca surrendered the city to <b>Azzone Visconti</b>, and Como became part of the Lordship of Milan, later the Duchy of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-como">Commune of Como</a></li><li><blockquote>Faction struggles between the Cavalcabò and their rivals weakened the commune, and in 1334 it submitted to <b>Azzone Visconti</b>, becoming part of the Lordship of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-cremona">Commune of Cremona</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1335 Lodi submitted to <b>Azzone Visconti</b> and became part of the Lordship of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-lodi">Commune of Lodi</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1332 Novara submitted to <b>Azzone Visconti</b> and became part of the Lordship of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-novara">Commune of Novara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.84,11.62&amp;year=1326" aria-label="Ferrara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4827 579 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Azzone Visconti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5277 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5277 849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.84,11.62&amp;year=1326">What was here in 1326? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
